course_description:
A practice-oriented introduction to Australian corporate and business associations law for commerce students. The course focuses on how business structures allocate rights, duties and risk, with particular emphasis on companies: incorporation and corporate personality; corporate finance fundamentals; governance and decision-making; directors’ duties; shareholder rights and remedies; and corporate distress/external administration. Students learn to work with primary sources (Corporations Act, cases, regulator guidance) and to communicate structured solutions to commercially realistic problems.

This course introduces students to the legal regulation of companies and develops student knowledge of important aspects of Australian corporations law. You will learn about the law concerning companies, including separate legal personality and limited liability, formation of a company, structure of and relationship between company organs, management and governance, directors’ duties, shareholder rights, corporate finance and external administration. In addition, you will be introduced to Partnership and the regulations on data protection and privacy, which govern the collecting, generating, storing, accessing, using or sharing data and information, in Australia.

Final grades will be based upon your level of understanding of and participation in the course, determined by reference to various assessment items including:

• 9 Assessable IL&AC Tutorial Submissions;

• One (1) In-Semester IL&AC Assignment;

• And One (1) Final IL&AC Exam
